From: Helder Correia <heldercorreia@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Windows: detect the GPU only for windows rendered by it

calcPosition() asks forcedScreenForGLWindow() for the screen of the GPU
for every top-level window. The first call runs GpuDescription::detect(),
which calls Direct3DCreate9() and GetAdapterIdentifier(). This loads the
Direct3D 9 runtime and the user-mode display driver right before the first
CreateWindowEx() of the process.

On NVIDIA systems the driver enumerates every installed locale while it
loads. Traces on two machines showed 170 ms and 201 ms inside the probe,
before the first window of a widgets application could be created.
On a third machine, creating the first window took 46 ms before this
change and 7-8 ms after it, averaged over 40 launches.

The workaround for QTBUG-50371 moves a window to the screen of the GPU so
that OpenGL renders on the right adapter. A window rendered by the raster
paint engine doesn't render on any adapter, so skip the detection unless
the surface is accelerated, as done elsewhere in this file.

---
diff --git a/src/plugins/platforms/windows/qwindowswindow.cpp b/src/plugins/platforms/windows/qwindowswindow.cpp
index 0c17d3b..8503f44 100644
--- a/src/plugins/platforms/windows/qwindowswindow.cpp
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/windows/qwindowswindow.cpp
@@ -676,7 +676,9 @@ static QPoint calcPosition(const QWindow *w, const QWindowCreationContextPtr &co
 {
     const QPoint orgPos(context->frameX - invMargins.left(), context->frameY - invMargins.top());
 
-    if (!w || w->type() != Qt::Window)
+    // Only windows rendered by the GPU need the screen of the GPU. Detecting
+    // the GPU loads its Direct3D 9 driver, which can take hundreds of ms.
+    if (!w || w->type() != Qt::Window || !windowIsAccelerated(w))
         return orgPos;
 
     // Workaround for QTBUG-50371
